---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Why Documentation Matters - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="documentation" subChapterId="why-it-matters">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Every team says they value documentation. Few keep it up to date, because for years
				documentation was a human convenience that lost the race against shipping code. AI
				changes the economics on both sides. It makes documentation much faster to produce,
				and much more valuable, because the AI reads your docs directly every time it works
				on your code.
			</p>

			<p>
				That shift is why we now recommend working{" "}
				<a href="/requirements-gathering/documentation-first-development" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">documentation-first</a>: you write or update the relevant document before you write the code, and that document becomes the thing both people and the AI build from. This chapter covers where those docs live, what to put in them, and the templates that make them fast to write. This page is about why the effort pays off.
			</p>

			<h3>You're Onboarding a New Hire, Hundreds of Times a Month</h3>

			<p>
				Every time you start a new AI conversation, you are onboarding a brand new team
				member. They are capable, but they know nothing about your project, your conventions,
				or why things are the way they are. Unlike a human hire you onboard once, you onboard
				this one hundreds of times a month, across everyone on the team. Your documentation
				is that onboarding. The better it is, the faster each of those new hires becomes
				useful.
			</p>

			<p>
				This holds across roles. Strategy people benefit when the AI understands your product
				positioning, competitive landscape, and decision history. Developers benefit when it
				understands your architecture, conventions, and technical decisions. The documents
				differ; the principle does not.
			</p>

			<h3>The Documentation Gap</h3>

			<p>
				Most projects tell the same story: a README that was accurate six months ago, some
				scattered comments, maybe a Confluence page nobody updates. The real knowledge lives
				in people's heads and moves through Slack messages and meetings.
			</p>

			<p>
				That mostly worked when only humans needed to understand the project. AI tools cannot
				attend your standup or recall the conversation about why the payment module uses a
				queue. They work with what is written down and reachable in the files they can read,
				plus whatever you type into the conversation.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Working With AI Tools">
				<p>
					Undocumented knowledge is now a bottleneck for the whole team, not just for
					onboarding new people. Every time the AI makes an avoidable mistake because the
					context was not written down, that is documentation debt showing up in your sprint.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Documentation-First Flips the Default</h3>

			<p>
				In most codebases the code is the only authoritative artifact, and the "why" behind
				it lives in people's heads and stale wiki pages. Documentation-first flips that. The
				decision records, feature specs, and design notes are the source of truth for intent,
				and the code is the implementation of what those documents describe. For any
				non-trivial change, the document comes first: you capture what you are building and
				why, then implement it with AI assistance in the same change.
			</p>

			<p>
				This matters more with AI in the loop, not less. An AI assistant starts every task
				with no memory of your past decisions. If the reasoning behind your architecture
				lives only in the code, the AI has to reverse-engineer intent from implementation,
				and it will sometimes guess wrong. If that reasoning is written down, it reads it and
				works within your constraints instead of inventing its own.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="success" title="What you get">
				<p>
					Durable context that survives team changes, a written trail of why decisions were
					made, and an AI assistant that follows your intent because it can read it. The
					document you write to think through a problem is the same document the AI uses to
					implement it.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>The Path of Least Resistance Has Shifted</h3>

			<p>
				Before AI, missing docs were survivable. You asked a colleague, read Slack history,
				or figured it out from the code. In practice most people skipped the docs and tapped
				someone on the shoulder, because it was faster.
			</p>

			<p>
				AI moves the fast path. Now the quickest way to get context is to ask the AI, and the
				AI answers from your documentation. When the docs are good, asking the AI beats
				interrupting a colleague and is more reliable than searching Slack. Documentation is
				no longer competing with human convenience. It <em>is</em> the convenience.
			</p>

			<p>
				There is a second pressure. AI lets smaller teams produce more code, faster, so each
				person now owns a larger surface area of the codebase than before. Nobody holds all
				of it in their head. Documentation is how both you and the AI get back up to speed on
				parts of the system you have not touched in months. The result is a direct,
				measurable effect on the quality of AI output across the team, on every interaction,
				every day.
			</p>

			<h3>You Don't Have to Boil the Ocean</h3>

			<p>
				Most existing projects have a documentation gap, and you do not close it all at once.
				Start with an agents file and a <code>docs/</code> folder, then backport decisions and
				feature specs as you touch each area. AI makes this cheap: it reads your codebase and
				produces first drafts far faster than writing them from scratch.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
						Example: generating an approach doc
					</div>
					<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
						"Read through the authentication system in src/auth/ and write a short approach
						document explaining how auth works in this project. Cover the login flow, token
						refresh, and how permissions are checked. Keep it under 300 words. Flag anything
						that looks unusual or that you're not sure about so I can clarify."
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
						Example: documenting an API
					</div>
					<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
						"Look at all the route handlers in src/routes/api/ and create a markdown table
						documenting each endpoint: method, path, description, required auth, and
						request/response shapes. Use the TypeScript types to determine the shapes."
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				The step you cannot skip is <strong>review</strong>. The AI describes what the code
				does, but it cannot know why you chose that approach or what trade-offs you weighed.
				Add the reasoning, correct inaccuracies, and cut anything that restates the obvious.
				Ten minutes reviewing a draft produces documentation that would have taken an hour to
				write cold.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Start with one document. Pick the part of your project that causes the most
					confusion or the most repeated AI mistakes, and document that first. One good
					approach document that prevents five bad AI interactions a week is worth more than
					a complete but shallow wiki.
				</p>
				<p>
					Once you have a few documents you are happy with, use them as the basis for a
					documentation skill. Point the AI at your best examples and tell it to follow that
					style, length, and structure for future docs. This keeps things consistent and
					reins in the AI's tendency to over-write.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<p>
				Next, we look at <a href="/documentation/where-to-document" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">where to put these docs</a> so both humans and the AI can find them.
			</p>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
